## E01 Twenty records from the open lists — QUEUED, next · Records · no strategy

Authority: owner direction 23 Oct 2026 ("combine the SEG open data list and the national
registers, verify the sources, show the real data"); this charter.

**Outcome.** The map lists at least twenty datasets. Each record states publisher, origin, access
mode, licence (name, redistribution, attribution) and kinds in the publisher's words, and each
origin was opened by a person on the day the record was written.

**Context today (verified 23 Oct, scaffold commit).** Nine records exist in `records/`
(equinor/volve, terranubis/f3-demo-2020, rmotc/teapot-dome, nopims/poseidon, nzpam/parihaka,
sodir/factpages, nlog/nlog, nsta/ndr, seg/open-data-wiki). `osd validate` passes. Licence
`redistribution` is `unknown` on five of them. The SEG wiki page cannot be fetched from the VPS
(HTTP 403, OSD-D9); it opens in an ordinary browser.

**The design the lane must map to.**
1. One record per dataset, never per file; a register (Sodir, NLOG, NDR, NOPIMS) is one record
   with `source-list` among its kinds plus one record per named dataset it carries that the SEG
   list names.
2. Candidates, in this order until twenty: the SEG open-data list entries whose origin still
   answers; Penobscot, Kerry 3D, Stratton, Groningen (NAM/NLOG), Netherlands F3 wells, Norne
   (NTNU), SEAM Phase I, Marmousi (synthetic, flagged in `notes`), Dutch and Danish (GEUS) registers,
   Geoscience Australia NOPIMS 3D surveys beyond Poseidon.
3. A dead origin is still a record: `known_issues` says "origin dead on <date>; last mirror
   <url>", and the record stays at L0. Dead links are the point of a verified list.
4. Licence fields: `unknown` when the publisher's page does not state it; never inferred from a
   country. `share_alike: true` whenever the licence is a BY-SA family.
5. Each record's `added` is the day the origin was opened by the author; the PR says who opened it.

**Acceptance.**
- `osd validate` reports `>= 20 records, 0 problems` (counted from `records/*/*.yaml`).
- Every record has `licence.redistribution` in {allowed, restricted, forbidden, unknown} and a
  `licence.url`; at most 8 of the twenty are `unknown` (hand count in the PR).
- `kinds` of every record intersect the Ophiolite E55 registry vocabulary (the schema enum).
- At least 12 records carry a `region.bbox` (the map's extent rectangles).
- The `verify` workflow comment on the PR lists every changed record with a level.
- Validity is three separate tests, each with a mutation, already in `tests/test_records.py`;
  the lane adds none unless the schema changes.

**Out of scope.** Outlines (E03), checksums (E02), mirrors (E07), any schema change (if one is
needed, stop and open a D item).

**Stop rules.** A publisher's page says redistribution is forbidden: record it, do not mirror,
continue. A licence page needs a login to read: `unknown`, note it, continue. More than three
candidates from the SEG list are dead: continue, they are records at L0.

**Ownership and dependencies.** Records lane; paths `records/**`. No dependency.

**Evidence to inspect.** `uv run osd status` output in the PR; the verify comment; a screenshot
of the map list showing the count.
## E03 Outlines from the data — QUEUED · Kernel · strategy at lane open · one Codex round

Authority: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`; owner direction 23 Oct ("display the outlines of known
available wellbores, seismic outlines on a map").

**Outcome.** Outlines on the map are derived from the data or the publisher's own register, with
the derivation named on every feature. Every wellbore and survey on the Norwegian and Dutch
shelves, and the five 3D surveys of the SEG list that are at L1, are drawn solid. No dashed
placeholder remains for a record at L3.

**Context today (verified 23 Oct).** Five outline files exist, all `approximate: true`, hand
placed. `schema/outline.schema.json` fixes the feature properties. The verifier marks
`outline-derived` fail when any feature is approximate. Sodir FactMaps serves WMS/WFS; NLOG
serves WFS and CSV downloads; the SEG surveys ship as SEG-Y whose trace headers carry
coordinates (verified for F3 in Ophiolite's E123 work; assumed for the others).

**The design the lane must map to.**
1. One extractor per derivation rule, each a function `(record, inputs) -> FeatureCollection`
   in `src/osd/outlines/<rule>.py`: `register-wfs` (Sodir, NLOG), `segy-hull` (trace-header
   convex hull, with `segyio`), `las-header` (wellhead lat/long or publisher CSV), `publisher-file`
   (a shapefile or GeoJSON the publisher ships).
2. A record names its rule and inputs in a new optional field `outlines: {rule, inputs}`; the
   extractor writes `outlines/<id>.geojson` with `approximate: false` and `source` naming the rule
   and the input address. Committed outlines are the contract; the extractor is rerun on demand.
3. Coordinates are transformed to EPSG:4326 with `pyproj` from the CRS the record states;
   a record without `region.crs` cannot reach L3 for SEG-Y-derived outlines.
4. Size: when the merged `outlines.geojson` exceeds 20 MB, the build also writes PMTiles via
   `tippecanoe` (E05 consumes it). The GeoJSON stays the GIS address.
5. Wellbores are Points with `uwi` or `publisher_id`; surveys are Polygons; 2D lines are
   LineStrings. Nothing else.

**Acceptance.**
- Sodir: the number of exploration wellbores in `outlines/sodir__factpages.geojson` equals the
  count on the Sodir wellbore table page on the day of the run, by hand, ±0 (recorded in the PR).
- NLOG: the number of boreholes equals the NLOG borehole count on its data page, by hand.
- `segy-hull` on the F3 volume yields a polygon whose area in EPSG:23031 is within 2 % of the
  survey's stated inline × crossline extent (computed by hand from the survey definition).
- Mutation: a shuffled trace-header fixture still yields the same hull (order independence),
  and a fixture with one outlier trace changes the hull (the test proves the hull reads the data).
- `osd verify` reports `outline-derived pass` for every record with an `outlines` field.
- No feature with `approximate: true` remains for sodir, nlog, f3, teapot, poseidon, parihaka.

**Out of scope.** Rendering (E05), tiling beyond the PMTiles write, any dataset whose files need a
login the lane does not have (record stays at L1 with a note).

**Stop rules.** A publisher's WFS has no licence statement for the outlines: stop, add a D item.
SEG-Y coordinates are in a unit the header does not declare: mark approximate, note it.

**Ownership and dependencies.** Coding lane; paths `src/osd/outlines/**`, `outlines/**`,
`schema/record.schema.json` (adds `outlines`, kernel bump to record/2 with a test that version-1
files still validate), `tests/test_outlines.py`. Depends on E02 (the level ladder it feeds).

**Evidence to inspect.** The hand counts in the PR; a screenshot of Norway with solid outlines;
`osd status` showing L3 for the six records.
## E04 STAC catalogue — QUEUED · Kernel · strategy at lane open · one Codex round

Authority: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` ("formats, all standard"); Ophiolite connector doctrine
(SDK or standard, never bespoke).

**Outcome.** The QGIS STAC browser plug-in, and any STAC client, opens the catalogue by one
address and lists collections and items with licence, level and access mode. The map, QGIS and
Ophiolite read one catalogue, not three.

**Context today (verified 23 Oct).** `build/catalog/collections.json` is STAC-shaped (type,
stac_version, id, license, extent, providers, links) but unvalidated and has no items and no
root catalog. `osd:level` and `osd:access` are bare fields with no extension schema.

**The design the lane must map to.**
1. Static STAC 1.0: `catalog.json` root → one `Collection` per record → one `Item` per outline
   feature (wellbore or survey), with the feature geometry and bbox; assets link to the origin
   page today and to mirror files when E07 adds them.
2. An `osd` extension schema at `schema/stac-osd.json` for `osd:level`, `osd:checked_at`,
   `osd:access`, `osd:redistribution`, `osd:derivation` (the outline `source`).
3. `license`: the SPDX id when the record has one, else `other` with a `license` link, per the
   STAC spec; never `proprietary` for a bespoke open licence.
4. Validation in `make check` with `stac-validator` against the core and the osd extension; a
   fixture of two collections and three items with a known-bad mutation each.
5. The GeoJSON `outlines.geojson` stays; items reference the same feature ids so a map click and a
   STAC item agree.

**Acceptance.**
- `stac-validator catalog.json --recursive` passes on the build output.
- Item count equals outline feature count (test).
- A record at L0 still yields a collection with zero items (no vacuous pass: a test asserts both
  the collection and its empty item list).
- QGIS 3.34 STAC browser lists the collections from a local `http.server` (screenshot in the PR).
- Mutation: removing `osd:level` from one item fails validation (shown and restored).

**Out of scope.** A STAC API (static only), mirrors, search endpoints.

**Stop rules.** The STAC extension process needs a published schema URL: use the repository's
raw URL and note it; do not block on a registered extension.

**Ownership and dependencies.** Coding lane; paths `src/osd/stac.py`, `src/osd/build.py`,
`schema/stac-osd.json`, `tests/test_stac.py`, `Makefile`. Depends on E03 (items need outlines).

**Evidence to inspect.** The validator output in CI; the QGIS screenshot.
## E05 Map that scales — QUEUED · Surface · no strategy

Authority: owner direction 23 Oct (the offentligdata.com example: toggles, key, search, detail);
`docs/ENGINEERING.md` proof bar for surface.

**Outcome.** A person can filter the map, share the link and the receiver sees the same view;
thousands of wellbores pan without lag; a visitor understands in one page what the levels mean
and how to open a layer in QGIS or Python.

**Context today (verified 23 Oct).** `site/` is one HTML, one JS, one CSS on MapLibre 4.7 over
OpenFreeMap Positron; chips per kind, a level select, extents and placeholder toggles, search,
a list, a detail panel with three cards, a key. State is in memory only. GeoJSON source only.
Headless screenshot works with Playwright and swiftshader; the basemap did not render in the
headless run (overlays did).

**The design the lane must map to.**
1. URL state: filters, selection and viewport in the hash; load restores it.
2. Sources: PMTiles via `pmtiles` protocol when `data/outlines.pmtiles` exists, else GeoJSON;
   wellbores clustered below zoom 7.
3. Stale levels: `checked_at` older than 30 days shows "stale" next to the level.
4. A `how.html` page: the ladder, the three cards, the licence rule ("the publisher's page is the
   authority"), the contribution path. Plain words, no marketing.
5. Theme: dark basemap when OSD-D3 names one; until then Positron.
6. Mobile: the panel is a bottom sheet under 640 px (exists; keep it working).
7. `tools/shot.mjs` (exists) gets Playwright as a devDependency instead of borrowing the Ophiolite
   web tree.

**Acceptance.**
- Open `#q=volve`, the list shows one dataset (screenshot).
- A hash with a kind filter and a selection, pasted in a second browser context, renders the
  same list count and panel title (Playwright test, `tests/site/`).
- 10,000 synthetic wellbore points (generated in the test) render at zoom 5 with clusters; the
  frame after `moveend` arrives under 100 ms in the Playwright trace.
- `how.html` exists and the three cards' snippets run as written (the Python one executed in CI
  against the local server).
- No console error on load (Playwright asserts).

**Out of scope.** Accounts, state beyond the URL, a server, analytics.

**Stop rules.** A basemap needs a key: do not add one; keep Positron and note it under OSD-D3.

**Ownership and dependencies.** Coding lane; paths `site/**`, `tools/shot.mjs`, `tests/site/**`,
`Makefile` (site targets only). No dependency; runs in parallel with E02 on disjoint paths.

**Evidence to inspect.** Two screenshots (desktop, phone width) and the Playwright run in the PR.
## E06 Retrieval by manifest — QUEUED · Kernel · strategy at lane open · one Codex round

Authority: owner direction 23 Oct ("the bridge to transfer/select assets from a map to a
retrieval"); Ophiolite exchange doctrine (intentional get, nothing applies itself).

**Outcome.** A person selects datasets or wellbores on the map, downloads one manifest, and
`osd get manifest.json` fetches the files from their origins (or mirrors, E07), verifies the
checksums the catalogue knows, and writes the attribution lines the licences require next to
the files. A Python reader opens the manifest without the CLI.

**Context today (verified 23 Oct).** No manifest format, no fetcher. The detail panel's third
card says retrieval is chartered. Records carry `origin.url` only; file-level addresses arrive
with E02's `files[]` and E04's item assets.

**The design the lane must map to.**
1. `osd.manifest/1`: JSON with `created`, `catalog_url`, `items[]` each `{item_id, record_id,
   assets[]: {href, bytes?, sha256?}, licence: {name, spdx, statement, share_alike}}`. Schema at
   `schema/manifest.schema.json`. No credentials, ever.
2. Site: a "Select" mode adds items to a basket; "Download manifest" writes the file client
   side. Items without resolvable assets (access `registration`/`request`) are included with
   `assets: []` and the origin page, so the manifest is also the to-do list.
3. `osd get <manifest> --dest <dir>`: streams each asset, verifies `sha256` when present, writes
   `ATTRIBUTION.md` from the licence statements, refuses to continue on a mismatch, resumes on
   rerun. Honest outcome per asset: fetched, verified, mismatch, needs-sign-in, origin-dead.
4. `osd.manifest.read(path)` in `src/osd/manifest.py` returns typed items; this is the interface
   Ophiolite's intake reads (E09).
5. Rights carried: every item's `licence.statement` is copied verbatim; `share_alike` is
   surfaced in the CLI summary ("2 items require derived work to carry CC BY-SA 4.0").

**Acceptance.**
- A manifest for two records validates against the schema; a mutated one (missing `licence`) fails.
- `osd get` on a fixture served locally with 3 assets: 3 fetched, 3 verified; corrupt one byte of
  one asset: exit non-zero, outcome `mismatch` for that asset, the other two untouched.
- `ATTRIBUTION.md` contains the F3 statement verbatim and names CC-BY-SA-4.0 as share-alike.
- Site: select two datasets, download, the file has two items (Playwright).
- Rerun after a partial failure fetches only the missing asset (test counts requests).

**Out of scope.** Mirrors (E07), seismic slices (E10), authentication to publisher portals,
decoding any file format.

**Stop rules.** A publisher's terms forbid automated download: that record's items get
`assets: []` and a note; continue.

**Ownership and dependencies.** Coding lane; paths `src/osd/manifest.py`, `src/osd/get.py`,
`src/osd/cli.py`, `schema/manifest.schema.json`, `site/app.js` (select mode only, claimed after
E05 merges), `tests/test_manifest.py`. Depends on E04 (item ids and assets).

**Evidence to inspect.** The strategy's test map; a terminal transcript of `osd get` on F3 with a
real origin, in the PR.
## E07 Mirror store and custody — QUEUED · Kernel · strategy at lane open · one Codex round

Authority: `DECISIONS.md` OSD-D6 (mirror policy); Ophiolite E35 (custody registry of every copy).

**Outcome.** Small datasets whose licence allows redistribution download from the catalogue at
full speed with a checksum, and every copy we hold has a custody record saying what it is, where
it came from, under which licence, and how to ask for its removal.

**Context today (verified 23 Oct).** Nothing is mirrored. Two Hetzner S3 buckets exist for the
Ophiolite program; none for this project, and its keys must never reach this one. Records carry
`licence.redistribution`; four of nine are `allowed`.

**The design the lane must map to.**
1. Eligibility is a function, tested: `redistribution == allowed` and estimated size under 20 GB
   (OSD-D6) and `origin` reachable at L2 with checksums. Nothing else is ever copied.
2. Layout: `s3://<bucket>/mirror/<record-id>/<sha256-prefix>/<filename>`, public read, no listing.
3. Custody record `build/custody/<record-id>.json`: files, sha256, bytes, copied_at, origin href,
   licence snapshot (name, url, text sha from E02), takedown contact (a repository issue template).
4. STAC assets (E04) gain a second href with `roles: ["mirror"]`; `osd get` prefers the mirror
   when its checksum matches the origin's.
5. Takedown: an issue with the template removes the files and marks the custody record
   `removed_at`; the catalogue keeps the record at its level with `assets` pointing at origin.
6. Cost line in the PR: bytes mirrored and the monthly storage and egress estimate at Hetzner
   prices, by hand.

**Acceptance.**
- Eligibility test: F3 (allowed, 1.3 GB) eligible; Volve (unknown) not; NDR (unknown, TB) not;
  a fixture with `allowed` and 25 GB not.
- Mutation: flipping the size cap to 30 GB makes the 25 GB fixture eligible (shown, restored).
- After mirroring F3: `osd get` for F3 reports every asset `verified` from the mirror href.
- The custody record validates against `schema/custody.schema.json`.
- Takedown dry run on the fixture removes the mirror href and writes `removed_at`.

**Out of scope.** Seismic chunking (E10), anything with `redistribution: unknown` even if the
licence looks permissive.

**Stop rules.** The bucket needs a key in CI: ask the owner for the repository secret (owner
action), continue with a local directory backend meanwhile.

**Ownership and dependencies.** Coding lane; paths `src/osd/mirror.py`, `schema/custody.schema.json`,
`.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/takedown.yml`, `tests/test_mirror.py`. Depends on E06 and OSD-D6; the bucket
and its key on E11.

**Evidence to inspect.** The cost line; the custody record for F3; the `osd get` transcript.
## E08 Curation that runs itself — QUEUED · Surface · no strategy

Authority: `CONTRIBUTING.md`; analogues named 23 Oct (Registry of Open Data on AWS, conda-forge).

**Outcome.** An outside contributor's record merges the same day with one human reading the
licence fields; a dead origin becomes an issue with the date it died; a visitor sees when each
record was last verified and by what.

**Context today (verified 23 Oct).** `CONTRIBUTING.md`, a PR template and a dataset issue
template exist. The `verify` workflow comments levels on records PRs. No CODEOWNERS, no dead-link
report, no status page, no outside contribution yet.

**The design the lane must map to.**
1. `CODEOWNERS`: `records/**` to the records maintainers, `schema/**` and `src/**` to the kernel
   maintainers.
2. Dead-link report: a dispatch of `verify` over all records ends with one issue per record
   that fell from L1+ to L0 since the last stored result, titled "origin dead: <id>", with the
   HTTP class and date; blocked (403) never opens an issue (OSD-D9).
3. `site/status.html`: the `osd status` table with `checked_at`, sortable, from `records.json`.
4. A "Suggest a dataset" issue is turned into a record PR by a maintainer with `osd new <url>`,
   which scaffolds the YAML from the template and the page title.
5. First outside contribution: the lane asks two named people from the Software Underground
   community to add one record each and records the elapsed time from PR open to merge.

**Acceptance.**
- Two outside PRs merged, each under 24 h from open to merge (dates in the PR descriptions).
- A fixture run where one record's fetcher returns 404 after a stored L1 opens exactly one issue
  (dry-run output in the test).
- `status.html` lists every record with level and date (screenshot).
- `osd new https://example.org/x` writes a file that fails validation only on the fields a human
  must fill (test asserts the exact problem list).

**Out of scope.** Automated licence detection, scheduled runs (R6: on demand), moderation tooling.

**Stop rules.** No outside contributor answers within a week: record it, merge the rest, leave
the acceptance line open in PROGRAM.md rather than faking a contribution.

**Ownership and dependencies.** Coding lane; paths `.github/**` (announced), `site/status.html`,
`src/osd/new.py`, `src/osd/report.py`, `tests/test_report.py`. Depends on E02 (stored results).

**Evidence to inspect.** The two PR links; the status screenshot.
